### Update the supplied form

If the form already contains JavaScript that submits the registration, update its API endpoint to the URL you copied. If it has only the HTML fields, the submission can be handled with JavaScript like this:

```javascript

const form = document.getElementById("registrationForm");

form.addEventListener("submit", async (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();

    const formData = new FormData(form);

    const registration = {
        name: formData.get("name"),
        email: formData.get("email"),
        phone: formData.get("phone")
    };

    try {
        const response = await fetch(
            "PASTE_YOUR_API_INVOKE_URL_HERE",
            {
                method: "POST",
                headers: {
                    "Content-Type": "application/json"
                },
                body: JSON.stringify(registration)
            }
        );

        const result = await response.json();

        if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error(result.error || result.message);
        }

        alert("Registration saved successfully!");
        form.reset();
        console.log(result);

    } catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
        alert("Registration failed: " + error.message);
    }
});
```

This example assumes the HTML form has `id="registrationForm"` and input elements named `name`, `email`, and `phone`. If your provided code uses different IDs or names, adapt the JavaScript rather than replacing the entire form.

Important: A browser-based form must be served from a suitable local or hosted web page. Opening an HTML file directly may cause browser-origin restrictions, depending on the browser and API configuration.

